#!/usr/bin/env bash
# kit-delivery - lists the artifacts, environments, and deployments the forge holds (kit verb: workflow)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# kit delivery [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# Draws what the forge says of the repository's delivery, a stage for each of Delivery's tabs in kittui that the forge
# answers. It changes nothing. The tabs releases and builds are kit flow's, as kit flow releases and kit flow builds
# print them. The stages, in order:
#
#   artifacts → environments → deployments
#
# kit --workspace=<workspace> delivery, the kit option before the verb, draws each stage across a workspace's members
# with checkouts, as kit workspace does: a group's name, or a repository's host and path, as kit workspaces names it;
# none names no workspace. Without it, the workspace is the repository here, and outside one, the last group used, as
# kit workspaces --current says. On a terminal, kit delivery opens Delivery in kittui, and kit delivery <stage> opens it
# on that tab, maximized; so do kit delivery releases and kit delivery builds. Without one, or with --text, it prints
# the stages as text; with --json or --jq, as JSON; a tab of kit flow, with a form, is refused, naming kit flow.
#
#   --text              prints the stages as text, even on a terminal
#   --json              the stages as one JSON object: stages, then each stage's document under its name, null for one
#                       that could not be read
#   --jq                the same JSON, formatted by jq for a person
#   --stages            prints the stages, one per line, in order, the same over any workspace
#   --marks             prints a mark per stage, as one JSON object, which changes when what the stage draws does:
#                       for each, the forge's own mark of its runs, as a run makes each
#   artifacts           the artifacts the runs kept, the newest 100 at most
#   environments        the deployment environments, each with what guards it
#   deployments         the deployments, the newest 50 at most
#   releases            a tab kit flow prints; on a terminal it opens in kittui, and with a form it is refused
#   builds              a tab kit flow prints; on a terminal it opens in kittui, and with a form it is refused
#
# kit delivery --stages
#
# kit delivery --marks
#
# kit delivery artifacts [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit delivery environments [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit delivery deployments [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit delivery releases [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit delivery builds [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# With --json, a stage prints {rows, errors}, and limited where the forge holds more than it read. A row is {key, …,
# links}: key is the row's own in its stage, and links gives where a value leads, by its field. Over a workspace, each
# row gains member, its host and path, and a member that could not be read is {member, error} under errors, while
# every other member still draws. A repository with no forge, or a forge without the stage's feature, gives no rows
# and no errors.
#
#   artifacts           {id, name, digest, size, run, age, expires}, keyed by its id: digest is the forge's, as
#                       sha256:…, null on GitLab and Azure DevOps, which show none; size in bytes; run the run that kept
#                       it; age seconds since it was made; expires when it goes, in seconds since the epoch, or null.
#                       The name links to its page. GitHub reads its 100 newest, GitLab the archives of its 100 newest
#                       successful jobs, Azure DevOps those of its 20 newest runs; where there are more, the document
#                       is limited, at how many it drew
#   environments        {name, protection, approvers, branches}, keyed by its name: protection the kinds of rule that
#                       guard it, as reviewers, wait, branches, or deployers; approvers who may approve, by login;
#                       branches those that may deploy there, a tag's as refs/tags/…, null for any. GitLab names no
#                       branches, and a GitLab whose tier, or caller, cannot read protected environments leaves
#                       protection and approvers null. The name links to its page. GitHub reads the branch policies
#                       of 20 environments that have their own, and the list is limited before a 21st
#   deployments         {id, environment, ref, sha, state, actor, age}, keyed by its id: state is success, failure,
#                       running, waiting, cancelled, or inactive; actor who made it; age seconds since. environment
#                       links to its row in environments, sha to the commit's page, and state to its log. Azure DevOps
#                       reads the deployment records of the 20 environments changed last, and names each one's ref,
#                       commit, and actor from its run among the 100 newest; where there are more, the document is
#                       limited, at how many it drew
#
# Exits 0 when the stages are drawn, 1 when a stage could not be read, after what was, and 2 on a usage error, or
# outside a repository's working tree with no workspace.
#
# see: modules/bash/apps.sh, modules/bash/apps/delivery.sh, modules/jq/apps.jq, docs/apps.md

set -o errexit -o nounset -o pipefail
# shellcheck source=modules/bash/1st.sh
source "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")/../modules/bash/1st.sh"
kit:enter
use apps
workspaces:misplaced delivery "$@" || exit $?
guard:arguments delivery "$@" || exit $?
apps:main delivery "$@"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
